{"name":"Blue Paisley","hex":"#2282a8","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"paisley","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A motif of a swirling droplet.","A soft woollen fabric having this motif."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made from this fabric, or marked with this design."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/paisley"}],"entries":[{"title":"Paisley (design)","blocks":[{"text":"Paisley or paisley pattern is an ornamental textile design using the boteh or buta, a teardrop-shaped motif with a curved upper end. Of Iranian/Persian origin, paisley designs became popular in the West in the 18th and 19th centuries, following imports of post-Mughal Empire versions of the design from India, especially in the form of Kashmir shawls, and were then replicated locally."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Shawl_LACMA_M.84.244.1.jpg/330px-Shawl_LACMA_M.84.244.1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paisley_(design)","wikidata":"Q937704"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect 